    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The implementation is complete and all tests pass. Here's a summary of what I did.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe change adds a **structured address form** (`addressLine1` + optional `addressLine2`) alongside the existing flat `address`, keeping the request contract backward-compatible.\n\n### API contract (`openapi.yml`)\n- Added `addressLine1` and `addressLine2` to the `OwnerFields` schema (so they appear on both request and response DTOs, since `Owner` extends `OwnerFields`).\n- Removed `address` from the `required` list so structured-only payloads pass Bean Validation, and documented that `address` on output is the composed, normalized string.\n\n### Domain & persistence\n- **`Owner`** model: added `addressLine1` / `addressLine2` columns and accessors.\n- Added `address_line1` / `address_line2` columns to all four schema files (hsqldb, h2, mysql, postgres) \u2014 tests run on hsqldb.\n\n### New single-responsibility component\n- **`AddressResolver`** centralizes the \"prefer structured, fall back to flat\" logic. It reuses the existing `AddressNormalizer` to canonicalize each supplied field, then:\n  - prefers a non-blank `addressLine1`, normalizing it and the optional `addressLine2`, and composes `address` = normalized line1 (`+ \" \" + normalized line2` when line2 is present);\n  - otherwise clears the structured lines and normalizes the flat `address`.\n  - It also exposes `hasStructuredAddress` / `hasFlatAddress` for validation.\n\n### Wiring\n- **`OwnerFieldsValidator`** now requires an address in *either* form (via `AddressResolver`) plus city \u2014 replacing the old flat-only check.\n- **`OwnerRestControllerV1`** uses `AddressResolver.resolve(owner)` on both create and update, replacing the direct one-off `AddressNormalizer` call (which is now encapsulated inside the resolver, so the controller no longer depends on `AddressNormalizer` directly).\n- The MapStruct `OwnerMapper` maps the new fields automatically by name in both directions.\n\nThe consumers the spec calls out (household hash, postcode validation, locality) read `city`/`postcode`, which are shared across both forms, so they already behave correctly.\n\nFull test suite: **2 run, 0 failures, BUILD SUCCESS**.",
